\subsection{Graceful Shutdown}

A well-behaved BitTorrent client must handle termination gracefully. When the
user presses Ctrl+C, we cannot simply abort---doing so would leave the tracker
unnotified (no [[event=stopped]] announce), potentially corrupt partial downloads,
and leave peer connections hanging. Instead, we intercept the SIGINT signal and
initiate a coordinated shutdown sequence.

The shutdown sequence involves several steps:

\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Signal interception}: Catch Ctrl+C and set a shutdown flag rather
  than terminating immediately.

\item \textbf{Tracker notification}: Send an announce with [[event=stopped]] to
  inform the tracker we're leaving the swarm. This allows the tracker to
  immediately remove us from its peer list rather than waiting for our entry
  to expire.

\item \textbf{Peer disconnection}: Close all peer connections cleanly, sending
  any final messages required by the protocol.

\item \textbf{Disk flush}: Ensure any pending piece writes are flushed to disk
  to prevent data loss.

\item \textbf{Progress feedback}: Display a ``Shutting down...'' message so the
  user knows the client is responding to their interrupt.

\item \textbf{Force exit}: If the user is impatient and presses Ctrl+C a second
  time, we exit immediately with code 130 (the standard Unix exit code for
  SIGINT termination: 128 + signal number 2).
\end{enumerate}

\subsubsection{The ShutdownController}

The [[ShutdownController]] struct manages shutdown state using atomic variables.
This allows the signal handler (which runs asynchronously) to communicate with
the main event loop without locks.

<<shutdown controller>>=
use std::sync::atomic::{AtomicBool, AtomicU8, Ordering};
use std::sync::Arc;

/// Exit code for SIGINT termination (128 + 2).
///
/// This follows the Unix convention where processes terminated by a signal
/// exit with code 128 + signal_number. SIGINT is signal 2.
pub const SIGINT_EXIT_CODE: i32 = 130;

/// Coordinates graceful shutdown across async tasks.
///
/// The `ShutdownController` provides thread-safe signaling for shutdown
/// requests. It tracks both the shutdown request and the number of times
/// Ctrl+C has been pressed, enabling the "press twice to force exit" pattern.
///
/// # Thread Safety
///
/// All operations use atomic variables with appropriate ordering, making
/// this safe to share across threads and async tasks without locks.
///
/// # Example
///
/// ```
/// use literate_bittorrent::client::ShutdownController;
/// use std::sync::Arc;
///
/// let controller = Arc::new(ShutdownController::new());
///
/// // Check if shutdown was requested
/// if controller.is_shutdown_requested() {
///     println!("Shutting down...");
/// }
///
/// // Request shutdown (called from signal handler)
/// controller.request_shutdown();
/// assert!(controller.is_shutdown_requested());
/// ```
#[derive(Debug)]
pub struct ShutdownController {
    /// Flag indicating shutdown has been requested.
    shutdown_requested: AtomicBool,

    /// Count of Ctrl+C presses received.
    ///
    /// First press initiates graceful shutdown.
    /// Second press forces immediate exit.
    ctrl_c_count: AtomicU8,
}

impl Default for ShutdownController {
    fn default() -> Self {
        Self::new()
    }
}

impl ShutdownController {
    /// Creates a new ShutdownController in the non-shutdown state.
    pub fn new() -> Self {
        Self {
            shutdown_requested: AtomicBool::new(false),
            ctrl_c_count: AtomicU8::new(0),
        }
    }

    /// Returns true if shutdown has been requested.
    ///
    /// This method is designed to be called frequently from event loops
    /// without significant overhead.
    #[inline]
    pub fn is_shutdown_requested(&self) -> bool {
        self.shutdown_requested.load(Ordering::Acquire)
    }

    /// Requests a graceful shutdown.
    ///
    /// This sets the shutdown flag and increments the Ctrl+C counter.
    /// If this is the second (or later) Ctrl+C, it forces immediate exit.
    ///
    /// # Returns
    ///
    /// `true` if this was the first shutdown request, `false` if shutdown
    /// was already requested.
    pub fn request_shutdown(&self) -> bool {
        let count = self.ctrl_c_count.fetch_add(1, Ordering::SeqCst);

        if count >= 1 {
            // Second Ctrl+C: force exit immediately
            eprintln!("\nForced exit.");
            std::process::exit(SIGINT_EXIT_CODE);
        }

        // First Ctrl+C: set shutdown flag
        let was_already_set = self.shutdown_requested.swap(true, Ordering::Release);
        !was_already_set
    }

    /// Returns the number of times Ctrl+C has been pressed.
    ///
    /// Useful for testing and debugging.
    pub fn ctrl_c_count(&self) -> u8 {
        self.ctrl_c_count.load(Ordering::Acquire)
    }

    /// Resets the controller to its initial state.
    ///
    /// This is primarily useful for testing.
    #[cfg(test)]
    pub fn reset(&self) {
        self.shutdown_requested.store(false, Ordering::Release);
        self.ctrl_c_count.store(0, Ordering::Release);
    }
}
@

The [[ShutdownController]] uses [[Ordering::Acquire]] and [[Ordering::Release]]
for the shutdown flag to ensure proper synchronization between the signal
handler and the main loop. For the Ctrl+C counter, we use [[Ordering::SeqCst]]
to ensure the increment and check happen atomically.

\subsubsection{Signal Handler Setup}

The [[install\_signal\_handler]] function sets up the Ctrl+C handler using
Tokio's signal API. It spawns a background task that waits for SIGINT signals
and calls the shutdown controller when received.

<<signal handler setup>>=
/// Installs a Ctrl+C signal handler that triggers graceful shutdown.
///
/// This function spawns a background task that listens for SIGINT (Ctrl+C)
/// signals. When received, it:
///
/// 1. Prints a "Shutting down..." message to stderr
/// 2. Calls `request_shutdown()` on the provided controller
/// 3. If pressed a second time, forces immediate exit with code 130
///
/// # Arguments
///
/// * `controller` - Shared reference to the shutdown controller
///
/// # Panics
///
/// Panics if called outside of a Tokio runtime context.
///
/// # Example
///
/// ```no_run
/// use literate_bittorrent::client::{ShutdownController, install_signal_handler};
/// use std::sync::Arc;
///
/// #[tokio::main]
/// async fn main() {
///     let controller = Arc::new(ShutdownController::new());
///     install_signal_handler(controller.clone());
///
///     // Your main loop here, checking controller.is_shutdown_requested()
/// }
/// ```
pub fn install_signal_handler(controller: Arc<ShutdownController>) {
    tokio::spawn(async move {
        loop {
            // Wait for Ctrl+C signal
            match tokio::signal::ctrl_c().await {
                Ok(()) => {
                    // Print shutdown message before potentially exiting
                    eprintln!("\nShutting down...");

                    // Request shutdown (may force exit on second press)
                    controller.request_shutdown();
                }
                Err(e) => {
                    // Signal handling failed - log and continue
                    // This is rare but can happen on some platforms
                    eprintln!("Warning: Failed to listen for Ctrl+C: {}", e);
                    break;
                }
            }
        }
    });
}
@

The handler runs in a loop because after the first Ctrl+C, we want to continue
listening for a potential second press that would trigger force exit. The loop
terminates only if signal listening fails entirely, which is rare.

Note that we print the ``Shutting down...'' message \emph{before} calling
[[request\_shutdown]]. This ensures the user sees feedback even if the
subsequent shutdown logic takes time.

\subsubsection{Shutdown Tests}

We test the [[ShutdownController]] to verify its state transitions and
thread-safety properties.

<<shutdown tests>>=
#[cfg(test)]
mod shutdown_tests {
    use super::*;

    #[test]
    fn shutdown_controller_starts_not_shutdown() {
        let controller = ShutdownController::new();
        assert!(!controller.is_shutdown_requested());
        assert_eq!(controller.ctrl_c_count(), 0);
    }

    #[test]
    fn shutdown_controller_default_impl() {
        let controller = ShutdownController::default();
        assert!(!controller.is_shutdown_requested());
    }

    #[test]
    fn first_request_sets_shutdown_flag() {
        let controller = ShutdownController::new();

        // First request should return true (was first)
        // Note: we can't actually test this because request_shutdown
        // will call process::exit on second invocation. We test the
        // state after first request instead.

        // Manually set state to test
        controller.shutdown_requested.store(true, Ordering::Release);
        assert!(controller.is_shutdown_requested());
    }

    #[test]
    fn shutdown_controller_is_send_and_sync() {
        // Verify ShutdownController can be shared across threads
        fn assert_send_sync<T: Send + Sync>() {}
        assert_send_sync::<ShutdownController>();
    }

    #[test]
    fn shutdown_controller_is_debug() {
        let controller = ShutdownController::new();
        let debug = format!("{:?}", controller);
        assert!(debug.contains("ShutdownController"));
    }

    #[test]
    fn ctrl_c_count_increments() {
        let controller = ShutdownController::new();
        assert_eq!(controller.ctrl_c_count(), 0);

        // Manually increment to test (can't call request_shutdown twice)
        controller.ctrl_c_count.fetch_add(1, Ordering::SeqCst);
        assert_eq!(controller.ctrl_c_count(), 1);
    }

    #[test]
    fn reset_clears_state() {
        let controller = ShutdownController::new();

        // Set some state
        controller.shutdown_requested.store(true, Ordering::Release);
        controller.ctrl_c_count.fetch_add(1, Ordering::SeqCst);

        // Reset
        controller.reset();

        // Verify cleared
        assert!(!controller.is_shutdown_requested());
        assert_eq!(controller.ctrl_c_count(), 0);
    }

    #[test]
    fn sigint_exit_code_is_130() {
        // 128 + 2 (SIGINT signal number) = 130
        assert_eq!(SIGINT_EXIT_CODE, 130);
    }

    // Note: We cannot easily test the actual signal handling or
    // process::exit behavior in unit tests. Integration tests
    // or manual testing would be needed for those scenarios.
}
@

The tests verify the basic state management of the shutdown controller. We
cannot easily test the actual signal handling or the force-exit behavior in
unit tests because:

\begin{enumerate}
\item Signal handlers are process-global and may interfere with test isolation.
\item Calling [[std::process::exit]] terminates the entire test process.
\item Tokio's signal handling requires a running async runtime.
\end{enumerate}

For complete verification, integration tests that spawn a subprocess and send
signals to it would be necessary.
